## A provider that is not there, and one whose DllMain fails

Two more states the standalone probe never entered, because it always had the
1.32.0 provider beside it. Both were run by swapping the provider DLL in the built
bundle (the 1.32.0 one was copied aside and put back afterwards; the bundle is
back to 195 MB), with the same switch:

  - bundle without a provider at all (the ai_pipeline_test.exe case):
      onnx-inference: v2 step: RegisterExecutionProviderLibrary
      onnx-inference: v2 step: register failed: Error loading
        "...\build\Release\onnxruntime_providers_cuda.dll" which is missing.
        (Error 126)
  - bundle holding the 1.20.1 provider, whose DllMain cannot initialise against
    the 1.32.0 core - the mismatch Resolve is in, where a provider's imports do
    not resolve:
      onnx-inference: v2 step: RegisterExecutionProviderLibrary
      onnx-inference: v2 step: register failed: Error loading
        "...\Contents\Win64\onnxruntime_providers_cuda.dll" But no dependency
        issue could be determined. (Error 1114: "Eine DLL-Initialisierungsroutine
        ist fehlgeschlagen.")

Both come back as an OrtStatus, the plug-in labels the session
`CPU (1 thread) - fallback: <that message>` and renders on the CPU. The suite
then exits 1, because its CUDA gate refuses a CPU fallback - that is the gate
working, not a plug-in failure.

So the registration step does not fast-fail on a missing library or on a library
that fails to initialise; the 0xC0000409 the probe could not explain is not in
any of these three states either.

Note on the code path: the full status text is printed by the V2 stage line
(`register failed: ...`), because the label's shortened message stops at the path
and never reaches the reason - that addition is what made the two lines above
readable at all.
